WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.920
senso intorno ad ipotesi di questo tipo e quindi è un semestre e una sfida difficile per

00:06.920 --> 00:16.420
il nostro Paese. Oggi quindi la nostra conferenza inizia con questo tema prioritario, la Costituzione.

00:16.840 --> 00:23.060
Domani invece dedicheremo i nostri lavori alla politica estera, che è un altro tema

00:23.060 --> 00:29.620
fondamentale e divideremo i lavori in gruppi sui Balcani, sul Mediterraneo, sulla Russia,

00:29.620 --> 00:36.500
sull'Iraq, che sono punti di grande interesse per l'Italia e per l'Unione Europea, ma che

00:36.500 --> 00:44.120
sono anche legati strettamente alla richiesta di riforma della Costituzione, e cioè in

00:44.120 --> 00:50.520
altre parole al rafforzamento di quel ruolo di politica estera e di sicurezza che è necessario

00:50.520 --> 00:56.920
perché l'Unione Europea giochi un ruolo importante internazionale. Quindi diciamo che queste due

00:56.920 --> 01:02.960
parti della nostra conferenza sono legate a filo doppio proprio per la logica con cui

01:02.960 --> 01:08.740
si tengono assieme una riforma istituzionale che di fatto crea questa capacità europea

01:08.740 --> 01:16.700
di agire come attore internazionale. Ecco, di fronte con queste considerazioni, io volei

01:16.700 --> 01:22.500
naturalmente ringraziare anche gli sponsor di questa nostra iniziativa, la Commissione

01:22.500 --> 01:27.020
di Bruxelles, la Compagnia di San Paolo, ma soprattutto il Ministero degli Esteri, che

01:27.020 --> 01:34.660
ci ha seguito molto, è qui rappresentato fra il resto da Ferdinando Nelli-Feroci, con cui

01:34.660 --> 01:42.580
abbiamo confezionato, diciamo, questa conferenza. E un grazie anche al circolo ufficiali, Palazzo

01:42.580 --> 01:48.340
Barberini, che ci ha ospitato per il calore con cui ci ha accolto, proprio in merito mi

01:48.340 --> 01:55.580
è tolto le parole. Grazie e passo la parola in ordine alfabetico, seguiamo, ma anche

01:55.580 --> 02:00.900
in ordine siccome gerarchico, perché come vicepresidente della Convenzione chiederei

02:00.900 --> 02:04.640
a Giuliano Matto di aprire la discussione. Grazie.

02:24.540 --> 02:40.120
Quanti minuti? 19 minuti. Ok, well, substantially Gianni, you said what has to be said. It's

02:40.120 --> 02:47.160
not perfect, the draft that we have produced is not perfect. I wonder if the Intergovernmental

02:47.160 --> 02:56.160
Conference is equipped to improve it or to worsen it. And I frankly think that they might

02:56.160 --> 03:03.600
more easily worsen it than improve it, which means that the shortcomings of the existing

03:03.600 --> 03:10.860
text are doomed to remain in my view. I'm very pessimistic on this, because I sound

03:10.860 --> 03:20.320
and their positions of national governments ready to do something in terms of changing

03:20.320 --> 03:27.900
what has been done and what they have in mind to change is on the wrong side. Of course,

03:28.200 --> 03:35.620
worsening or improving depends on what you think. Let's say in my view, in my view I'm

03:35.620 --> 03:44.940
firmly convinced to pick one point that giving each of our member states a commissioner with

03:44.940 --> 03:53.000
full rights is not an improvement. I understand that the commission, for reasons that frankly

03:53.000 --> 04:04.320
I don't share, has a different position. But in my view, in my view, there is a limit

04:04.320 --> 04:11.060
to the efficiency of the commission that doesn't go beyond a certain number of commissioners

04:11.060 --> 04:25.140
holding a portfolio without slicing the existing portfolios into pieces that requires the commission

04:25.140 --> 04:34.160
not to go beyond something that is around 15 full members. And this was exactly what

04:34.160 --> 04:55.120
not Giscard but Jacques Delors advised us to do. Now, let me state this point. In our

04:55.120 --> 05:04.420
view, let's say human beings may be part of the structure. Only 15 full members of it,

05:04.480 --> 05:11.400
but other commissioners coming from other countries can be appointed and can be useful

05:11.400 --> 05:19.360
as long as they are something such as delegated commissioners. When people say, well, there

05:19.360 --> 05:25.360
are so many national governments with 30, 40 ministers, the French government is quoted

05:25.360 --> 05:33.300
as a government having 40 ministers, but at this point any French who knows how things

05:33.300 --> 05:40.400
are in his or her country objects, but only 15 are full ministers, the other are delegated

05:40.400 --> 05:47.460
ministers. And this is what we expect the commission to be. Consider that initially

05:47.460 --> 05:55.440
we called, we named the other commissioners, delegated commissioners. I made a negotiation

05:55.440 --> 06:03.000
with the national governments in the convention. They accepted, they accepted all of them,

06:03.020 --> 06:10.700
all of them, 15 full members on a strictly equal rotation basis. At that point they

06:10.700 --> 06:17.000
asked me to give the others a name that they could sell in their countries. And it was

06:17.000 --> 06:23.320
not a matter of selling, not a matter of substance. And they objected to the delegated commissioner

06:23.320 --> 06:30.060
because it seemed something more hierarchically subordinated than something else. And therefore

06:30.060 --> 06:38.680
at the end we called the first class commissioners European commissioners and the other ones

06:38.680 --> 06:45.700
commissioners. At this point there are reactions in some countries and they say, but what

06:45.700 --> 06:51.860
are these gentlemen going to do hanging around? They are not even delegated to do something.

06:52.980 --> 07:00.660
Now the response is read the draft and the draft says that the president of the commission

07:00.660 --> 07:08.150
is responsible toward the European Parliament for the activities of these commissioners,

07:08.720 --> 07:16.630
which means that the president will give them missions to each of them and he or she

07:16.630 --> 07:23.590
will be responsible for their activities toward the parliament. It is, I must say at the end

07:23.590 --> 07:32.390
of the story, the same kind of commission that the commission had in mind, the two tiers

07:32.390 --> 07:41.890
commission, which means that in a two tiers commission not necessarily all of them have

07:41.890 --> 07:50.150
the voting rights around. Now this is a crucial point in my view. I am convinced that with

07:50.150 --> 08:00.550
the commission having at the end of the story more than 30 full members with voting rights

08:00.530 --> 08:13.430
the commission would become an intergovernmental arena, somehow reducing its potential as the

08:13.430 --> 08:25.270
necessary engine in the European interest. For several member states changing the draft

08:25.950 --> 08:33.270
in this sense would be improving it. So we have to be very clear on what we consider improving

08:33.670 --> 08:42.010
or worsening. I can expect this kind of thing to have quite a significant pressure during

08:42.010 --> 08:49.410
the intergovernmental conference, which would be a pity because the commission has been

08:49.410 --> 08:55.170
strengthened throughout the process. I must say I am one of those who have always thought

08:56.230 --> 09:04.710
that a long term president is useful for the council. So let's say Herman is one that

09:04.710 --> 09:12.770
was opposing it and wanted the prize for it. So if I have to swallow the president, this

09:12.770 --> 09:19.970
is Herman's argument, what do you give me? And he got something because he got for the

09:19.970 --> 09:30.150
commission significant improvement in terms of tasks, not just the monopoly in legislative

09:30.150 --> 09:38.350
initiative, not just the almost monopoly in executive functions, but also the yearly

09:38.350 --> 09:47.130
and multi yearly planning of union activities which is going to be organized on the basis

09:47.130 --> 09:55.190
of the commission initiative, plus the European council meetings jointly prepared by the general

09:55.190 --> 10:02.030
affairs council and by the commission, plus at the end the president of the commission

10:02.550 --> 10:13.430
jointly with the president of the council supervising the preparation of the European

10:13.430 --> 10:21.930
council and also the elimination of that clause by which there was an incompatibility between

10:21.930 --> 10:27.230
the presidency of the European council and the presidency of the commission, which is

10:27.230 --> 10:34.370
much less than the clause some of us had in mind to give the union a single president

10:34.370 --> 10:44.110
in let's say 10 years, but at least it opens the way to it. Therefore the commission is

10:44.110 --> 10:55.230
somehow safe in its role and the institutional balance has been preserved, but should the

10:55.230 --> 11:05.870
arena I was envisaging this in my view would be a significant worsening in the future of

11:05.870 --> 11:16.090
the institutional balance itself. Qualified majority, I don't think the IGC will do something

11:16.090 --> 11:25.210
to widen the cases of qualified majority indeed. I don't think the IGC will do something to

11:25.230 --> 11:34.410
widen the cases of qualified majority. At the end of the story there are some cases,

11:34.870 --> 11:44.470
substantially some cases and you have several governments playing against here or there.

11:47.770 --> 11:55.030
Taxation distorting competition could be somehow decided upon by qualified majority

11:55.030 --> 12:01.910
voting, but you don't have the UK only, you also have Denmark, you also have Ireland,

12:02.190 --> 12:10.610
you also have Sweden, you also have others. Social policies, it's not the UK, it's Germany

12:11.090 --> 12:22.050
that is firmly opposing qualified majority in relation to the now Article 137 and they

12:22.050 --> 12:33.810
don't want to go beyond Nice. France is already resenting the limited ventilation

12:33.810 --> 12:44.130
that we introduced in agricultural policy, giving something to legislation and much more

12:44.130 --> 12:55.230
to Council's regulations. I have already seen amendments that reduce the something given

12:55.230 --> 13:03.570
to European legislation and should this amendment be accepted sooner or later, legislation

13:03.570 --> 13:10.950
would just have the smoke and all of the meat would be and would remain for Council's regulation.

13:10.950 --> 13:20.930
What can I expect from the IGC that France drops its objections to widening the role

13:20.930 --> 13:29.170
of qualified majority and co-decision in agricultural policy in exchange for Germany widening

13:29.170 --> 13:37.470
the area of social policies that go to co-decision and qualified majority? This is something

13:37.470 --> 13:48.630
unpredictable and therefore to conclude with, I don't think that there will be leftovers

13:48.630 --> 13:56.910
formally, I am convinced that there will be leftovers substantially, so I don't expect

13:56.910 --> 14:06.190
the IGC to formally decide that unsettled issue will go to a further appointment with

14:06.190 --> 14:13.790
either a Convention or an IGC. I expect them to remain where we have remained if not

14:13.790 --> 14:26.230
worse in this area. I include in this area also amendments of the text because you are

14:26.230 --> 14:34.170
right Gianni, it would be so reasonable and this was something that Herman and I proposed

14:34.170 --> 14:44.390
jointly, we accept unanimity for any amendment that imply further transfers of sovereignty

14:44.390 --> 14:53.050
from member states to the Union, while any other amendment which simply implies reorganisation

14:53.050 --> 15:00.910
at the European level of existing competences and therefore does not reduce or touch upon

15:01.870 --> 15:07.810
what remains in the hands of member states, why shouldn't this be approved by a majority

15:07.810 --> 15:16.530
vote? Possibly by a super qualified majority voting, what's the reason? Really there is

15:16.530 --> 15:23.630
no reason, I ignore now the fact that they want the procedure of international treaties

15:23.630 --> 15:30.890
to apply also for the future therefore not the European Council but the IGC, not the

15:30.910 --> 15:37.070
national parliaments approving but the national parliaments ratifying which is really something

15:37.070 --> 15:45.630
that for me as a lawyer means treaty, cannot mean constitution but independently also of

15:45.630 --> 15:54.050
it, why don't they accept majority voting when their remaining competences are not

15:54.050 --> 16:00.310
touched upon by the amendments? The funny thing is that at the end of this process,

16:00.310 --> 16:07.070
this one, we might find ourselves with an overall more cumbersome procedure for amendments

16:07.070 --> 16:14.090
than the existing one because nowadays we have several clauses that can be modified

16:14.090 --> 16:23.030
by quasi autonomous or autonomous procedures and as we say in technical jargon by which

16:23.030 --> 16:30.290
national parliaments are not involved, unanimity is necessary anyway but the unanimity of

16:30.290 --> 16:36.190
the European Council could be enough now to change several clauses of the existing

16:36.190 --> 16:44.410
treaties. Paradoxically you understand how times change, one of the clauses that can

16:44.410 --> 16:51.250
be changed only by decision of the European Council without involving national parliaments

16:51.250 --> 17:00.270
is the clause on the number of commissioners that has become the hottest issue of the European

17:00.290 --> 17:08.990
Council of all, so I don't expect this to change, what do I expect? I do expect in

17:08.990 --> 17:22.050
a few years the union to face some deep crisis because they cannot decide on the basis of

17:22.050 --> 17:30.270
unanimity. Assuming unanimity as the method for their decisions, the European Council

17:30.290 --> 17:39.530
simply means that they cannot decide even more in an enlarged Europe and therefore after

17:39.530 --> 17:48.250
a while they will realize perhaps by themselves that if they really want to exercise the competencies

17:48.250 --> 17:59.450
they wanted at the European level but unanimously decided upon, they will have to change. In

18:02.070 --> 18:11.690
the last three months of the convention this was not even mentioned anymore by national

18:11.690 --> 18:17.670
parliaments because it was thought that this was a positive result which will help us

18:17.670 --> 18:22.870
in the future to come to a good relationship between national parliaments and European

18:22.870 --> 18:28.970
parliaments in order to fulfill our responsibility to control the executive branches. Thank you

18:28.970 --> 18:36.870
very much. Thank you, Elmer Brock, for your evaluation of the result of the convention

18:37.570 --> 18:45.550
and also for your sharp judgment on the role of the IGC. In any case, may I now, before

18:45.550 --> 18:53.270
addressing to the audience and ask for some questions or comments, may I ask Ferdinando

18:53.270 --> 19:04.710
Nell'Efferoci to comment about this first round of introductions without anticipating

19:05.210 --> 19:10.970
the priorities of the Italian presidencies which will be part of the next session. Thank

19:10.970 --> 19:18.050
you. Gianni, thank you. I think I can limit my remarks to very brief ones because I'd

19:18.050 --> 19:25.470
like to say a little more on how we would like to proceed in the preparation of the

19:25.470 --> 19:31.490
IGC and in the conduct of the IGC in fact. Broadly speaking, I'd like to confirm what

19:31.490 --> 19:38.690
has already been said at official level by several representatives of the Italian government

19:38.690 --> 19:45.570
including Vice President Fini who has been an active member of the convention, including

19:45.570 --> 19:52.150
Presidente Berlusconi, including Foreign Minister Mr. Flattini, and this is to confirm

19:52.150 --> 19:59.150
that we really very much appreciate the results achieved by the convention. We think that

19:59.150 --> 20:07.930
we are in the presence of a historical development. If we look back into the past way of proceeding

20:07.930 --> 20:15.550
in treaty reforms and treaty revisions, we are now faced with an extraordinary crisis

20:16.170 --> 20:23.830
of innovation and we are also very much satisfied with the overall articulation of the draft

20:23.830 --> 20:31.590
constitutional treaty which has been submitted to the government in Tessaloniki. Maybe as

20:31.590 --> 20:37.830
many others, members of parliaments, representatives of governments, we would have had preferences

20:37.830 --> 20:42.970
here and there. There would have been solutions that we would like to see more than others

20:42.970 --> 20:50.550
reflected in the draft treaty, but as a whole we consider that it's a very good basis

20:50.550 --> 20:59.970
for the work of the intergovernmental conference. I fully share the appreciation which was made

20:59.970 --> 21:07.990
a few minutes ago by Presidente Amato when he noted that there is a risk in reopening

21:07.990 --> 21:15.750
the carefully balanced text of the treaty dispositions and the risk is that we may

21:15.750 --> 21:22.690
fall backwards more than move forwards. We would carefully study all the alternatives

21:22.690 --> 21:27.530
that we will be faced with when launching the IGC, but certainly we will carefully

21:27.530 --> 21:34.890
have in mind this note of caution. In more general terms, I think that I fully share

21:34.890 --> 21:44.010
the individual points that were made by President Brock and that all together we share his

21:44.010 --> 21:48.990
points of appreciation for the text. But having said that, I think I would prefer to leave

21:48.990 --> 21:55.790
it for my next intervention, some more detailed remarks on how we intend to proceed further

21:55.790 --> 22:00.890
on on our way towards the organization of the intergovernmental conference. Thank you,

22:00.890 --> 22:10.470
Alvaro. Ok, thank you very much. Now I address to the audience for questions and comments.

22:10.730 --> 22:17.750
Of course there is a mobile phone. Yes, please, could you come here? Alvaro de Vasconcelos

22:17.750 --> 22:27.510
from the Instituto de Estudios Strategicos of Lisbon. Yes, thank you very much. I want

22:27.510 --> 22:34.810
to make a comment and put a question to the panel. The comment is that I have the feeling

22:34.810 --> 22:42.810
that there was real progress in making the European integration more democratic, more

22:42.810 --> 22:51.590
transparent, more close to the citizens. Let's say if you look from outside, in Europe we

22:51.590 --> 22:58.870
are better now. Integration is moving forward. My question is the following. If this is true

22:58.870 --> 23:05.970
for Europe, it's not the same thing in relation to the ability of the European Union to project

23:05.970 --> 23:14.490
its values in the international scene. It's clear about what you said, but also just reading

23:15.730 --> 23:22.990
the constitution, that even the premise that came from the convention are not in this text.

23:24.010 --> 23:30.630
We have no majority voting as it was proposed in the Franco-German proposal. On the fence

23:30.630 --> 23:38.770
there is not really great progress. We don't have a clause of solidarity, clear clause

23:38.770 --> 23:45.770
of solidarity. So we have not really progressed on the ability to act internationally. So

23:46.130 --> 23:53.510
now coming to the question, is this the lessons you took from the Iraqi crisis? You spoke

23:53.510 --> 24:01.530
as a parliamentarian, I spoke as a citizen. And looking to your work, I ask myself, have

24:01.530 --> 24:07.230
you done your work based on the Iraqi crisis to make the European Union more able to act

24:07.230 --> 24:14.370
internationally? Or it was just the opposite, that the Iraq crisis made you more timid,

24:15.130 --> 24:20.870
less able to make the reform that was asked? Because looking to the debate in the convention

24:20.870 --> 24:26.430
and the result, it seems that there is two moments. Before the Iraq crisis, things seemed

24:26.430 --> 24:32.710
to go forward on the foreign policy and defense. And after the Iraq crisis, to opposite to

24:32.710 --> 24:37.530
the Iraq crisis, we have no lessons of Europeans in the streets that ask for more Europe in

24:37.530 --> 24:43.390
the demonstrations all over Europe. We don't have that. More Europe in international affairs.

24:44.010 --> 24:48.430
And it's a demand from outside Europe too. It's not only in Europe. If we go to North

24:48.430 --> 24:53.070
Africa, to the Middle East, to Latin America, to Africa, people say we need more Europe,

24:53.270 --> 24:56.750
not less Europe in the international arena. Why it is not there?

24:56.750 --> 25:04.610
Thank you. May I suggest to collect some questions and comments and then go back to the panel?

25:05.130 --> 25:10.130
I have another person there. Can you introduce yourself, please?

25:10.150 --> 25:15.170
My name is Rafał Trzaskowski. I'm from the European Center in Poland and I have a question

25:15.170 --> 25:21.830
to Mr. Edmund Brock. You have said that what parliamentarians achieved shouldn't change,

25:22.010 --> 25:26.230
have no right to change, and it's very difficult to disagree with that statement. But wouldn't

25:26.230 --> 25:32.390
you agree that hadn't there been an IGC plan, the achievements of the convention wouldn't

25:32.390 --> 25:40.390
be so far reaching? In other words, if you talk to many of the participants of the convention,

25:40.670 --> 25:47.250
parliamentarians included, some of them are not satisfied with the draft treaty. And

25:47.250 --> 25:53.230
so it seems that some of them have adopted a strategy, let's not spoil the celebration

25:53.230 --> 25:58.030
and let's wait for the IGC to change certain things that we don't like. You have said

25:58.030 --> 26:02.710
that there will be no big changes during the IGC. What changes do you envisage, if any?

26:02.910 --> 26:03.750
Thank you.

26:04.130 --> 26:10.870
Thank you. Other questions? We have a very timid audience here, not the convention, but

26:10.870 --> 26:13.850
the audience. Please. Yes, please.

26:14.430 --> 26:18.410
Dominik Hanf from the College of Europe in Bruges. This one?

26:18.410 --> 26:19.010
Yeah, okay.

26:19.430 --> 26:26.530
My question is concerning the adoption of the new constitution. All these matters on the

26:26.530 --> 26:31.670
adoption and the later revision of the constitution by a qualified majority have not really been

26:31.670 --> 26:38.410
discussed, but there will be a problem of fact that what will happen if one or two or three

26:38.410 --> 26:47.970
member states will not adopt the new constitution. So I know that the treaty, or the draft

26:47.970 --> 26:52.130
treaty, will have a problem and take political consequences, but my question is what will

26:52.130 --> 26:53.670
be the political consequences?

26:54.370 --> 26:57.970
Okay, thank you. Another question here and then there.

26:59.370 --> 27:00.490
The question is the following.

27:01.210 --> 27:03.710
Your name, please, to introduce yourself.

27:04.270 --> 27:10.050
Sorry, my name is Jan Willems. I'm the Belgian ambassador to the Quid Inan here in Rome.

27:11.350 --> 27:20.110
Why did the convention not suggest to the IGC that the end result, the constitution,

27:20.950 --> 27:28.650
would enter into force with less than a new unanimous ratification in the different member

27:28.650 --> 27:35.230
states? Because we can face the result and the risk is greater that one country would

27:35.230 --> 27:41.250
block the entry into force of this new constitution, which would be tragic.

27:42.710 --> 27:45.670
Thank you very much, Ambassador. Another question there.

27:46.150 --> 27:51.330
My name is Brendan Donnelly from the Federal Trust in London. Can I put a question arising

27:51.330 --> 27:55.390
from what Elmar Brock said about foreign policy, but I'd like comments if I may from everyone

27:55.390 --> 28:02.270
on that subject. Elmar Brock spoke as if the most important reason why a relatively cautious

28:02.270 --> 28:07.670
text on the question of foreign policy emerged was British opposition. It's widely believed

28:07.670 --> 28:12.810
in the United Kingdom, and I'd like a comment on this if I may, that while the British government

28:12.810 --> 28:18.610
is the most outspoken in its opposition to a more daring approach to foreign policy,

28:18.970 --> 28:23.750
in fact at least the French government and in some moods the German government has more

28:23.750 --> 28:28.690
sympathy with that view than is always publicly said. Now, whether that's right or wrong,

28:28.690 --> 28:33.890
can I just make the further point that British public and probably British elite opinion will

28:33.890 --> 28:40.010
never be convinced of any different proposition until what Elmar said was a possibility does

28:40.010 --> 28:43.870
happen. If you want to convince the British that they're the only people who are standing

28:43.870 --> 28:47.830
in the way of a more bold foreign policy, then it's up to others to take the lead.

28:51.350 --> 28:54.950
Other questions? Yes, please.

28:56.130 --> 29:04.970
General Pozzi, retired from the Italian Army, member of the Istituto D'Affari Internazionali.

29:05.310 --> 29:17.530
What about the security and defense? What will be the provisions in that sector? I would

29:17.530 --> 29:24.930
like to know if it is, of course, is in the agenda, but what ideas are going to be in

29:24.950 --> 29:27.730
the agenda that will be constructed in the end? Thank you.

29:28.230 --> 29:31.850
Thank you. Other questions? Questions can be also addressed in Italian.

29:35.210 --> 29:40.610
I'm Paolo Calzini of Milano University. How do you assess the fact that there has been

29:40.610 --> 29:46.510
so little debate and participation in terms of public opinion in Europe around the question

29:46.510 --> 29:49.030
of the Constitution? Thank you.

29:49.550 --> 29:57.490
Okay. I would suggest to go back to the two main speakers and ask them to answer or to

29:57.490 --> 30:04.210
comment or to conclude their reasoning here by thanking them, of course, for their patience

30:04.210 --> 30:11.130
and having succeeded to resist this hot climate. Please.

30:12.810 --> 30:22.890
Well, first of all, when you say that public opinion was somehow far from the Convention,

30:23.050 --> 30:30.890
you say something that is only partially true and much less than you think. First, you

30:30.890 --> 30:36.430
have to compare the attention given by public opinion to the Convention with the attention

30:36.430 --> 30:45.450
given to IGCs, because this is the only alternative, the benchmark. For the first time, if you

30:45.450 --> 30:55.230
look at this from this angle, we had an enormous debate compared with the past, a transparent

30:55.230 --> 31:02.930
debate that we never had in the past on modifications of the treaties. Please give a look at the

31:02.930 --> 31:10.090
side of the Convention and give a look at that part of the side that is named Forum.

31:10.950 --> 31:21.310
Under Forum, you have all the associations, groups and centers of research and others

31:21.870 --> 31:32.170
that took part in the debate by sending contribution, by interacting with us, by taking part in

31:32.170 --> 31:39.650
meetings, because we also had meetings with the representatives of this group. They are

31:39.650 --> 31:48.810
hundreds and hundreds, and not just the traditional lobbies that are based in Brussels and that

31:48.810 --> 31:55.530
of course show up whatever happens in Brussels, but also associations and groups from all

31:55.530 --> 32:04.430
the existing member states and the new member states. Some of them, unbelievable, because

32:04.430 --> 32:13.350
I did never expect the association for the safeguard of the right to nudity to have something

32:13.350 --> 32:22.990
to suggest, which if they suggested something, nothing appeared in the text, I suppose, except

32:22.990 --> 32:30.550
I hope the Charter takes care also of nudity, I don't know. But anyhow, there was quite

32:30.550 --> 32:39.910
an attention, so please don't say as usual, well, the citizens were absent. This is not

32:39.910 --> 32:48.710
correct. I don't expect the attention of let's say millions and millions of people to go

32:48.710 --> 32:56.930
to an event such as the Convention. I expect those citizens who tend to be active to have

32:56.930 --> 32:59.990
an opportunity to interact, to have a sense of responsibility, to have a sense of responsibility

